Thanks — I can help. First, two quick clarifying points so I calculate exactly what you need:

1. You listed cash = $5.2M, runway = 30 months, burn = $72K/month. Those three numbers don’t line up: 5.2M / 72K ≈ 72 months, not 30. Which is correct (cash and burn, or the 30-month runway)? If runway is calculated under different assumptions (e.g., planned hiring / seasonality / one-time items), tell me those adjustments.
2. To compute accurate CAC and LTV I need a few company inputs (if you don’t have all of them I can show scenarios):
   - Number of customers (total paying customers) or customers at quarter end
   - Number of new customers acquired in the quarter (or month)
   - Sales & marketing spend for the period used to compute CAC (quarter or year)
   - Average revenue per account (ARPA) — monthly or annual (if you only have total revenue, I can derive ARPA if you give customer count)
   - Gross margin (percent) — SaaS typically 70–80% but can vary
   - Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime

If you want, give me those numbers and I’ll compute exact CAC, LTV, payback, and LTV:CAC for your current period.

Below are the formulas, a worked example with plausible assumptions, recommended targets, and a short finance narrative you can adapt.

Key formulas
- CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period)
- ARPA (monthly) = (Monthly recurring revenue) / (Number of customers)
- Monthly churn rate = fraction of customers lost per month
- Customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn
- LTV (per customer) = ARPA_monthly * gross_margin * (1 / monthly_churn)
  (Equivalently LTV = gross_margin * ARPA_monthly * avg_lifetime_months)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPA_monthly * gross_margin)
- LTV:CAC = LTV / CAC

Quick inputs we can derive from your provided revenue
- Revenue last quarter = $670K → average monthly revenue ≈ $223.3K

Worked example scenarios
(assume gross margin = 75% unless you tell me otherwise)

A. If ARPA (monthly) = $1,000
- Implied customers = 223
- If monthly churn = 2% → avg lifetime = 50 months
  - LTV = 1,000 * 0.75 * 50 = $37,500
- If CAC = $10,000
  - Payback = 10,000 / (1,000 * 0.75) ≈ 13.3 months
  - LTV:CAC = 37,500 / 10,000 = 3.75

B. If ARPA (monthly) = $5,000 (more enterprise)
- Implied customers = 45
- If monthly churn = 1% → avg lifetime = 100 months
  - LTV = 5,000 * 0.75 * 100 = $375,000
- If CAC = $50,000 (enterprise sales)
  - Payback = 50,000 / (5,000 * 0.75) ≈ 13.3 months
  - LTV:CAC = 375,000 / 50,000 = 7.5

C. If ARPA (monthly) = $2,000 and monthly churn = 1.5% (avg lifetime ≈ 66.7 months)
- Customers ≈ 112
- LTV = 2,000 * 0.75 * 66.7 ≈ $100,000
- If CAC = $20,000 → payback ≈ 13.3 months, LTV:CAC = 5

Notes on the examples
- Many SaaS benchmarks: LTV:CAC >= 3 is healthy; CAC payback <= 12 months is strong for growth-stage; gross margin ideally > 70% for SaaS unit economics.
- Enterprise companies tolerate higher CAC and longer paybacks if LTV:CAC is high and churn is low.
- Small differences in churn dramatically change LTV (because lifetime = 1/churn), so accurately measuring churn is crucial.

If you only have revenue (670K last quarter) but not customer counts or S&M spend, here’s how to proceed quickly
- If you give me either customer count or ARPA, I can compute the other.
- If you give me total S&M spend for the quarter (or last 12 months) and new customers in that period, I’ll compute CAC directly.
- If you only want a sense of “what CAC can be” given runway and cash: we can calculate the maximum sustainable CAC given your growth targets and available cash (but we’ll need target new customers or growth rate).

Suggested finance narrative (short, slide-ready)
- “Our current MRR (~$223K/month) and ARR trends show [insert growth rate]. With a gross margin of ~X% and monthly churn of Y%, our customer LTV is approximately $Z. Our average CAC (quarterly S&M spend ÷ new customers) is $A, giving an LTV:CAC of B and a CAC payback of C months. These unit economics indicate [strengths/risks — e.g., healthy LTV:CAC >3 and acceptable payback, or CAC payback >12 months requiring S&M efficiency improvements].”
- Next steps: tighten churn measurement, reduce sales cycle or S&M inefficiencies to lower CAC, or increase gross margin/upsell programs to lift LTV.

What I can do next (pick one)
- If you paste these inputs I’ll compute exact metrics: total customers, new customers (quarter), S&M spend (quarter or FY), gross margin, churn (monthly/annual).
- Or I can produce a sensitivity table (LTV and LTV:CAC) across a range of ARPA and churn values you pick.
- Or I can draft the slide text and a 1-page memo you can include in the internal materials.

Which option would you like? If you want numbers computed now, please provide:
- total paying customers (or ARPA),
- new customers last quarter (or month),
- S&M spend last quarter (or annual),
- gross margin %,
- churn (monthly or annual).

