I can help — I’ll show the exact formulas, the numbers you can plug in, example scenarios to illustrate ranges, and a suggested finance narrative. First, a quick check: you gave three items that don’t align arithmetically, so please confirm definitions (I’ll explain below).

What I need from you to compute exact CAC and LTV
- Sales & marketing spend (period you want to use — e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)  
- Number of net new customers acquired in that same period  
- Number of active customers (or average customers) and/or average revenue per customer (ARPC) — monthly or annual preferred  
- Gross margin % (contribution margin after COGS for those customers)  
- Churn rate (monthly or annual customer churn)  

If you don’t have some of these, I’ll show how to estimate.

Key formulas (SaaS-style/unit-economics)
- Revenue per month (MR) = Quarterly revenue / 3 = $670,000 / 3 = $223,333 / month  
- ARPU (monthly) = MR / number_of_active_customers
- CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (new customers acquired in same period)
- Gross contribution per customer per month = ARPU * gross_margin
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly_churn_rate
- LTV (gross-margin based) = ARPU * gross_margin / monthly_churn
  (equivalently = gross contribution per month * lifetime)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU * gross_margin)
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC — target >= 3 is common guidance for healthy scalable growth

Examples — plug-and-play scenarios
(quarterly revenue = $670K → monthly revenue ≈ $223,333)

Example assumptions (illustrative):
- ARPU (monthly) = $1,000 → customers ≈ 223
- Gross margin = 75% (typical for SaaS; change if different)
- Monthly churn scenarios: 3%, 5%, 10%

Compute LTV:
- If churn = 3% → lifetime ≈ 33.3 months → LTV = 1,000 * 0.75 / 0.03 = $25,000
- If churn = 5% → LTV = 1,000 * 0.75 / 0.05 = $15,000
- If churn = 10% → LTV = 1,000 * 0.75 / 0.10 = $7,500

Example CAC and ratios (assume S&M this quarter = $200,000 and new customers this quarter = 25 → CAC = $8,000):
- CAC = $200,000 / 25 = $8,000
- LTV:CAC at 3% churn = 25,000 / 8,000 = 3.1 (good)
- LTV:CAC at 5% churn = 15,000 / 8,000 = 1.9 (weak)
- CAC payback months = 8,000 / (1,000 * 0.75) = 8,000 / 750 = 10.7 months (independent of churn)

Sensitivity highlights (what changes LTV vs what changes payback)
- LTV is very sensitive to churn and ARPU (reducing churn or raising ARPU greatly increases LTV)
- CAC payback depends on CAC and gross contribution per month (ARPU * gross_margin) — churn does not affect payback
- LTV:CAC combines both effects — you need both reasonable payback and high lifetime

Runway inconsistency — please confirm
- You said cash = $5.2M, runway = 30 months, burn = $72K/month. If burn is net cash burn, runway = cash / burn = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72 months, not 30.  
- If runway = 30 months, implied net burn = 5,200,000 / 30 ≈ $173K/month.  
Please confirm whether:
  - $72K/month is operating cash burn excluding revenue?  
  - The 30-month runway was calculated including some other assumptions (planned hires, one-time spends, or conservative adjustments)?  
I’ll align the narrative once you confirm.

Suggested finance narrative for internal materials (concise)
- Current position: “We have $5.2M in cash and a stated runway of 30 months (note: please confirm burn definition). Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈$2.68M run-rate).”
- Unit-economics summary: show CAC, ARPU, gross margin, monthly churn, LTV, CAC payback months, and LTV:CAC. Use a 3-line summary and a 1-slide sensitivity table showing LTV across churn scenarios (2–10%) and ARPU scenarios.
