I can help — I’ll show the formulas, what inputs I need from you, and a few illustrative scenarios so you can see how CAC and LTV change with different assumptions. Quick note first: there’s an inconsistency in the numbers you gave that I want to confirm.

Important inconsistency to check
- You said cash = $5.2M, burn = $72K/month, and runway = 30 months. If runway is simply cash / burn, 5.2M / 72K ≈ 72 months, not 30. Possible explanations: your $72K is gross burn (not net), runway is calculated using a different burn number (e.g., including planned spending increases), or runway is net of expected revenue. Can you confirm how you measure burn and runway?

What I need from you to calculate CAC and LTV (or tell me if you want me to run examples)
- Active customer count (total paying customers)
- New customers acquired in the period you want to measure (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)
- Sales & marketing spend for that same period (including marketing, sales salaries/commissions, agency fees)
- Average Revenue per Account (ARPA) — monthly or annual (or total revenue and number of customers, I can compute ARPA)
- Gross margin (contribution margin after direct costs of service)
- Customer churn (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime

If you don’t have all of the above I can run sensitivity scenarios using reasonable SaaS assumptions — see examples below.

Formulas (clean, actionable)
- CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (New customers acquired in same period)
- ARPU/ARPA (monthly) = (Monthly revenue) / (number of active customers)
- Average lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn rate (if you have annual churn, convert: monthly_churn ≈ 1 - (1 - annual_churn)^(1/12))
- LTV (gross-margin-adjusted) = ARPU_monthly × gross_margin × average_lifetime_months
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC — target benchmark for healthy SaaS is roughly >= 3:1 (and payback period < 12 months ideally)

Quick calculations using your provided revenue (illustrative only)
- Revenue last quarter = $670K → implied monthly revenue ≈ $670K / 3 = $223,333 → implied ARR ≈ $2.68M.
I’ll run 3 illustrative scenarios below. These are just examples — replace inputs with your actuals for precise numbers.

Example assumptions (Scenario A — “good” SaaS metrics)
- Active customers = 450
- Monthly ARPU = 223,333 / 450 ≈ $496
- Gross margin = 80%
- Monthly churn = 2% → average lifetime ≈ 50 months
- S&M spend last quarter = $300,000; new customers acquired last quarter = 150 → CAC = $300K / 150 = $2,000
- LTV = 496 × 0.8 × 50 ≈ $19,840
- LTV:CAC ≈ 19,840 / 2,000 ≈ 9.9 (excellent)

Scenario B — “conservative” (higher churn, higher CAC)
- Same ARPU ($496), gross margin 70%, monthly churn 5% → lifetime 20 months
- LTV = 496 × 0.7 × 20 ≈ $6,950
- If CAC = $3,000 (higher marketing spend / fewer new customers) → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.32 (below target)

Scenario C — “early-stage / higher ARPA” (fewer customers, bigger contracts)
- Active customers = 150 → ARPU = 223,333 / 150 ≈ $1,489/month
- Gross margin 75%, monthly churn 1.5% → lifetime ≈ 67 months
- LTV = 1,489 × 0.75 × 67 ≈ $74,800
- If CAC = $10,000 (higher enterprise sales cost) → LTV:CAC ≈ 7.48 (still healthy for enterprise)

