I can definitely help — but first a couple of important clarifications, then I’ll give you the exact formulas, examples, and a ready-to-use finance narrative you can drop into your materials. After that I can run the numbers for you if you supply a few missing inputs.

1) Quick inconsistency to resolve
- You said: cash = $5.2M, runway = 30 months, burn = $72K/month.
- Those three can’t all be true simultaneously if “runway” = cash / net burn:
  - If cash is $5.2M and runway is 30 months → implied net burn ≈ $5.2M / 30 ≈ $173.3K/month.
  - If cash is $5.2M and burn = $72K/month → implied runway ≈ $5.2M / $72K ≈ 72 months.
  - If runway = 30 months and burn = $72K/month → implied cash ≈ 30 * $72K = $2.16M.
Please confirm which numbers are correct and whether “burn” is gross burn (total cash outflows) or net burn (outflows minus operating revenue). Also confirm whether runway is calculated using net burn.

2) What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- New customers acquired in the period you want to measure (quarter or month). If you only have total customers and new customers not tracked, give the best estimate.
- Sales & marketing spend over that same period (include paid marketing, agency fees, salaries for S&M, sales commissions tied to acquisition — exclude retention-only costs if you want acquisition CAC).
- Revenue granularity: you gave revenue last quarter = $670K (good). If this is ARR-trending or one-off, let me know.
- Gross margin (or cost of goods sold %). For SaaS, typical gross margin is 70–90% — tell me your actual or use a default.
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) OR average customer lifetime (in months or years). If you don’t have churn, tell me whether customers are subscription-based and typical contract lengths.
- Any one-time onboarding or implementation costs per customer (if material).

3) Formulas and explanation (use these in your deck)
- CAC (customer acquisition cost)
  CAC = Total acquisition-related S&M spend during period / Number of new customers acquired during same period

- LTV (SaaS subscription, unit-economics form)
  LTV = ARPU_monthly × Gross margin (%) / Monthly churn rate
  where ARPU_monthly = Revenue_per_month / #customers

  Equivalently (annual):
  LTV = ARPA_annual × Gross margin / Annual churn rate

- LTV : CAC ratio — key benchmark
  LTV : CAC = LTV / CAC
  Healthy target: ≥ 3x (depends on stage); < 1 means you lose money acquiring customers.

- CAC payback period
  CAC payback = CAC / (ARPU_monthly × Gross margin)
  (gives months to recover CAC from gross contribution)

4) Worked examples using your revenue (I’ll show 3 scenarios — change inputs to match your reality)
- Base numbers derived from your quarter revenue:
  - Revenue last quarter = $670K → monthly revenue ≈ $223,333.

Example A — assume 2,000 customers, gross margin 75%, monthly churn 2%, S&M spend last quarter $200K and new customers last quarter = 300
  - ARPU_monthly = $223,333 / 2000 = $111.67
  - CAC = $200,000 / 300 = $667
  - LTV = 111.67 × 0.75 / 0.02 = $4,187.63
  - LTV : CAC ≈ 4,187.63 / 667 ≈ 6.3x (very healthy)
  - CAC payback = 667 / (111.67 × 0.75) ≈ 7.97 months

Example B — lower ARPU/higher churn: 1,000 customers, gross margin 70%, churn 4% monthly, S&M spend $200K, new customers 250
  - ARPU = 223,333 / 1000 = $223.33
  - CAC = 200,000 / 250 = $800
  - LTV = 223.33 × 0.7 / 0.04 = $3,903.3
  - LTV:CAC ≈ 4.88x
  - CAC payback ≈ 800 / (223.33 × 0.7) ≈ 5.1 months

Example C — high CAC scenario: same revenue, 1,500 customers, churn 3%, gross margin 70%, S&M spend $500K, new customers 200
  - ARPU = 223,333 / 1500 = $148.89
  - CAC = 500,000 / 200 = $2,500
  - LTV = 148.89 × 0.7 / 0.03 = $3,472.3
  - LTV:CAC ≈ 1.39x (problematic)
  - CAC payback ≈ 2,500 / (148.89 × 0.7) ≈ 24 months (long)

These examples show how sensitive unit economics are to churn, gross margin, and S&M spend.

5) Suggested finance narrative (copy/paste-ready)
- Short summary:
  "Our current quarterly revenue is $670K (≈ $223K/month). To evaluate unit economics, we focus on CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and CAC payback. Inputs required are S&M spend and new customers acquired, gross margin, and churn. Preliminary scenarios show LTV:CAC ranges from ~1.4x (unsustainable) to >6x (very strong), driven primarily by churn and S&M efficiency. Target benchmarks for Summit Software: LTV:CAC ≥ 3x, CAC payback ≤ 12 months, and monthly churn < 2–3% for SaaS subscriptions."
- Findings & actions:
  - "If our monthly churn is ≤ 2% and gross margin ≥ 70%, we can support CACs in the mid-hundreds while maintaining >3x LTV:CAC."
  - "If churn rises above 3–4% or S&M spend per new customer exceeds ~$1,500, payback stretches beyond 12 months and LTV:CAC falls below target — prioritize retention and improvement in onboarding/activation."
  - "Recommended next steps: validate S&M attribution (paid vs organic), compute CAC by channel, run cohort LTVs, and model 3 scenarios (conservative / base / aggressive) for board materials."

6) What I can do next for you (pick any)
- If you give me:
  - new customers last quarter (or monthly),
  - S&M spend for that period,
  - gross margin %
  - monthly churn %
  I’ll compute CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and payback and produce a short slide-ready table and bullets.

- I can also:
  - produce sensitivity tables (showing LTV:CAC under varying churn and CAC),
  - draft a 1-page narrative for the board,
  - estimate runway impact if you adjust CAC (e.g., increase S&M to grow MRR).

Which of the missing inputs above can you provide now? If you’re unsure about churn or gross margin, tell me if you want me to use typical SaaS defaults (gross margin 75%, monthly churn 2%) and I’ll run a baseline model.

