I can help — I’ll show the formulas, the data I need from you, and a few realistic scenario tables so you can pick the narrative that best fits Summit Software. First, quick summary of what’s missing and what I can compute now:

What I have from you
- Cash: $5.2M
- Runway: 30 months
- Burn: $72K / month
- Revenue last quarter: $670K (=> monthly revenue ≈ $223.3K; annualized ≈ $2.68M)

What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV exactly
- Sales & Marketing spend in the period you want to measure (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)
- Number of NEW customers acquired during that same period
- Total number of active customers (or customers at period end)
- Gross margin (percent) on revenue (SaaS typical: 65–85%)
- Churn (annual or monthly) — if you don’t have churn, I’ll use assumptions

Key formulas
- CAC = (Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (New customers acquired in that period)
- ARPU (monthly) = (Revenue in period) / (number of customers) normalized to months
  - e.g., monthly ARPU = (quarterly revenue / #customers) / 3
- Monthly gross margin per customer = monthly ARPU * gross_margin%
- Monthly churn (if given annual churn) = 1 - (1 - annual_churn)^(1/12)
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly_churn
- LTV = monthly gross margin per customer * customer lifetime (months)
- LTV / CAC and CAC payback months = CAC / (monthly gross margin per customer)

Benchmarks (common targets)
- LTV/CAC >= 3 (best practice)
- CAC payback < 12 months (target; <18 months acceptable for some enterprise models)
- Gross margin > 70% for SaaS product companies

Scenario examples (illustrative — changeable based on your real inputs)
Assumptions used for these examples:
- Monthly revenue = $223,333
- Gross margin = 75% (example)
- We show three ARPU tiers (derived from different total customer counts)
- We show three churn scenarios (annual churn = 30%, 10%, 5%)

Per-customer monthly ARPU examples (derived from monthly revenue)
- If total customers = 200 → monthly ARPU ≈ $1,116.67
- If total customers = 500 → monthly ARPU ≈ $446.67
- If total customers = 1,000 → monthly ARPU ≈ $223.33

Compute monthly gross margin per customer = ARPU * 75%:
- 200 cust ARPU → GM/month ≈ $837.50
- 500 cust ARPU → GM/month ≈ $335.00
- 1,000 cust ARPU → GM/month ≈ $167.50

Customer lifetime (months) for each annual churn:
- 30% annual → ~34 months
- 10% annual → ~114 months
- 5% annual → ~234 months

LTV (GM/month * lifetime):
- ARPU $1,116.67 (200 customers)
  - 30% churn → LTV ≈ $28.5K
  - 10% churn → LTV ≈ $95.7K
  - 5% churn → LTV ≈ $196.4K
- ARPU $446.67 (500 customers)
  - 30% → LTV ≈ $11.4K
  - 10% → LTV ≈ $38.2K
  - 5% → LTV ≈ $78.3K
- ARPU $223.33 (1,000 customers)
  - 30% → LTV ≈ $5.7K
  - 10% → LTV ≈ $19.1K
  - 5% → LTV ≈ $39.0K

Example CAC and payback implications
- Suppose CAC = $5,000 and monthly GM per customer = $375 (equivalent to ARPU $500, GM 75%).
  - Payback months = 5,000 / 375 ≈ 13.3 months
  - If lifetime LTV = $42.9K (10% churn example) → LTV/CAC ≈ 8.6 (excellent)
- If CAC = $10,000 with same GM:
  - Payback ≈ 26.7 months (too long)
  - LTV/CAC ≈ 4.29 (still healthy if you can tolerate long payback, but cash strain)

What this means for Summit Software’s finance narrative
- With 30 months of runway you have room to invest in growth, but you must manage CAC payback so you don’t accelerate cash burn. If CAC payback > runway or >12–18 months, growth spending can quickly deplete cash.
- Focus areas for improving unit economics:
  - Reduce churn (big leverage on LTV). Cutting annual churn from 10% → 5% roughly doubles LTV.
  - Increase ARPU (upsells, packaging, enterprise pricing) directly increases LTV and shortens CAC payback.
  - Improve marketing efficiency to lower CAC (better targeting, channel mix).
