Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — but first a quick flag: the three numbers you gave (cash $5.2M, runway 30 months, burn $72K/month) are inconsistent with each other. Which of these is correct?

- If burn = $72K/month, runway = 5.2M / 72K ≈ 72 months (not 30).
- If runway = 30 months, implied net burn = 5.2M / 30 ≈ $173.3K/month (not 72K).
- Your revenue last quarter $670K → monthly revenue ≈ $223.3K.

Please confirm which burn/runway figure is correct. I’ll proceed once you confirm, or I can show calculations assuming one of them.

What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV exactly
- Current number of customers (or MRR split by customer segments) OR new customers acquired in the period you want to analyze
- Sales & Marketing spend for the period (quarter or trailing 12 months)
- Period you want CAC for (last quarter, LTM, etc.)
- Gross margin (or COGS %)
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime
- Whether revenue last quarter includes one-time deals that should be excluded

If you don’t have all of those, I can run scenarios with reasonable assumptions — see examples below.

Key formulas (straightforward, use these in your materials)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing spend in period / Number of new customers acquired in same period
- ARPA (monthly) = Monthly revenue / number of customers
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly churn_rate (for constant monthly churn)
- LTV (SaaS, gross-margin-adjusted) = ARPA * gross_margin % / monthly_churn
- Payback months = CAC / (ARPA * gross_margin)
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC

Quick worked examples (using your revenue = $670K last quarter → monthly rev ≈ $223,333)
I’ll show three customer-count scenarios to illustrate sensitivity. Assume gross margin = 75% (changeable) and show churn 2% and 4% monthly.

1) If you have 200 customers:
- ARPA/month = 223,333 / 200 = $1,116.67
- LTV (GM75%, churn2%) = 1,116.67 * 0.75 / 0.02 ≈ $41,875
- LTV (GM75%, churn4%) ≈ $20,937

2) If you have 500 customers:
- ARPA = 223,333 / 500 = $446.67
- LTV (75%, 2%) ≈ $16,750
- LTV (75%, 4%) ≈ $8,375

3) If you have 1,000 customers:
- ARPA = 223,333 / 1,000 = $223.33
- LTV (75%, 2%) ≈ $8,375
- LTV (75%, 4%) ≈ $4,187

Example CAC and payback (illustrative)
- Suppose S&M last quarter = $300K and new customers last quarter = 100 → CAC = $3,000.
- For ARPA $446.67 (500 customers case) and GM 75%:
  - Monthly gross contribution per new customer = 446.67 * 0.75 ≈ $335
  - Payback months = 3,000 / 335 ≈ 9 months
  - LTV/CAC (with 2% churn) = 16,750 / 3,000 ≈ 5.6x (very healthy)

Benchmarks to use in the finance narrative
- LTV:CAC > 3x is generally considered healthy for growth-stage SaaS; >5x is excellent but can mean under-investing in growth.
- Payback < 12 months is attractive to VCs and corporate finance; <18 months can still be okay depending on unit economics and growth goals.
- Lower churn dramatically increases LTV — prioritizing retention often yields the highest leverage.

Suggested finance narrative bullets for internal deck
- “Current cash: $5.2M. Based on [confirmed burn], runway is [X] months; we need to align this with revenue and planned hiring/spend.”
- “Last quarter revenue = $670K (monthly ≈ $223K). With current customer base of [N], ARPA = $[X].”
- “Our LTV (Gross-margin adjusted) is $[LTV]; CAC is $[CAC]; LTV:CAC = [ratio] and payback period = [months].”
- “Key levers: lower churn by X% → increases LTV by Yx; increase S&M efficiency to reduce CAC; or adjust pricing to raise ARPA.”
- “Recommendation: reconcile burn/runway numbers, get S&M spend and new customer counts for the last 12 months, and run sensitivity table (churn 1–5%, GM 70–85%).”

Immediate next steps I can take for you
- If you give me: #customers (current), new customers (last quarter or LTM), S&M spend (quarter or LTM), gross margin, churn → I will compute CAC, LTV, payback, and produce a sensitivity table and a short narrative paragraph you can paste into your materials.
- Or, tell me which of your burn/runway numbers is correct and whether you want calculations for last quarter or trailing-12-months — I’ll propose a version to include in your deck.

Which data do you want me to use (and please confirm the correct burn/runway)? I’ll run the exact calculations and produce the narrative slide text.

