Key formulas and definitions
- Monthly revenue = quarterly revenue / 3. (Your quarterly revenue $670K → monthly ≈ $223,333.)
- CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) = Total sales & marketing spend over period / Number of new customers acquired in that same period.
- ARPU (average monthly revenue per user) = Monthly revenue / average number of customers in month.
- Monthly churn rate = customers lost in month / customers at start of month.
- Customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn rate.
- Gross margin per customer (monthly) = ARPU × gross margin % (use gross margin on revenue, not EBITDA margin).
- LTV (simplified, gross-margin based) = gross margin per customer × customer lifetime
  equivalently LTV = (ARPU × gross margin %) / monthly churn rate.
- LTV : CAC = LTV / CAC.
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU × gross margin %).

Recommended targets (benchmarks)
- LTV:CAC ratio: ≥ 3x is a common rule-of-thumb for SaaS (higher is better).
- CAC payback: < 12 months is healthy for aggressive growth; 12–24 months acceptable depending on stage.
- Gross margin: SaaS usually ~70–90% (use your gross margin on subscription revenue).

Example scenarios (illustrative — I’ll tailor once you give actual data)
Base inputs (from your data where possible)
- Monthly revenue = $670K / 3 = $223,333.

