- New customers in quarter: 50 (so CAC denominator = 50)
- S&M spend in quarter: $300,000 → CAC = 300,000 / 50 = $6,000
- Average customers = 200 → ARPU monthly = 223,333 / 200 = $1,116.7
- Gross margin = 75% → gross margin per customer = 1,116.7 × 0.75 = $837.5/month
- Monthly churn = 2% → lifetime = 1 / 0.02 = 50 months
- LTV = 837.5 × 50 = $41,875
- LTV:CAC ≈ 6.98x
- CAC payback = 6,000 / 837.5 ≈ 7.2 months

Scenario B — higher churn (worse retention)
- Monthly churn = 5% → lifetime = 20 months
- LTV = 837.5 × 20 = $16,750
- LTV:CAC ≈ 2.8x (below target)
- CAC payback still ≈ 7.2 months

Scenario C — lower ARPU (smaller customers)
- Average customers = 500 → ARPU = 223,333 / 500 = $446.7
- Gross margin @75% = 335/month
- With churn 2% (lifetime 50 months): LTV = 335 × 50 = $16,750
- If CAC still $6,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.8x (again below target)

What I need from you to compute your real CAC / LTV
- Period to analyze (last quarter? last 12 months?)
- Total sales & marketing spend over that period (total and ideally broken down by channel)
- Number of new customers acquired in the same period
