2) Formulas (short and clear)
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing Spend / Number of New Customers (for same period)
- ARPU (monthly) = (Revenue for period / number of months in period) / Total Customers
  - Using your quarterly revenue $670K -> monthly revenue ≈ $670K / 3 = $223,333
- Contribution per customer per month = ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin%
- Average customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly_churn_rate
- LTV = Contribution_per_month * Average_customer_lifetime
- Payback period (months) = CAC / Contribution_per_month
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC

3) Benchmarks / targets (typical SaaS guidance)
- Healthy LTV:CAC >= 3x (higher if capital-efficient; >= 3 is common for growth-stage SaaS)
- Payback period ideally < 12 months for high growth; up to 18–24 months can be okay for lower-growth, capital-lite.
- Gross margin for SaaS often 70–90% (after hosting + support costs but before S&M).

4) Worked examples (useful to show sensitivity). I use your revenue to derive ARPU where needed.

Assumptions common to both examples:
- Quarterly revenue = $670,000 -> monthly revenue ≈ $223,333

Example A — higher ARPU / low churn (growth-friendly)
- Total customers = 800
- New customers last quarter = 100
- S&M spend last quarter = $250,000
- Gross margin = 75% (0.75)
- Monthly churn = 1% (0.01)

Calculations:
- CAC = 250,000 / 100 = $2,500
- ARPU_monthly = 223,333 / 800 ≈ $279.17
- Contribution/month = 279.17 * 0.75 ≈ $209.38
- Lifetime = 1 / 0.01 = 100 months
- LTV = 209.38 * 100 ≈ $20,938
- LTV:CAC ≈ 20,938 / 2,500 ≈ 8.4x
- Payback = 2,500 / 209.38 ≈ 12.0 months

Interpretation: Strong unit economics; payback right around 12 months and LTV:CAC >> 3.

Example B — lower ARPU / higher churn (stress case)
- Total customers = 2,500
- New customers last quarter = 400
- S&M spend last quarter = $300,000
- Gross margin = 60% (0.60)
- Monthly churn = 5% (0.05)

