I can help — I’ll show the right formulas, flag a couple of inconsistencies in the numbers you gave, and then run a few example LTV / CAC scenarios so you have concrete figures to put into the finance narrative. After that I’ll list the exact inputs I need from you to compute your real CAC & LTV.

Quick note on the numbers you gave
- Cash = $5.2M and runway = 30 months imply implied net burn = 5.2M / 30 ≈ $173k/month.
- You also said burn rate = $72k/month. Those two can’t both be describing the same “net cash burn” metric. Which is correct? Or is $72k your operating (OPEX) burn while the 30-month runway already factors in expected revenue/proceeds, one-time items, or a projected change in burn? Clarify and I’ll use the correct burn for narrative/runway math.

Useful baseline fact from your data
- Revenue last quarter = $670k → average monthly revenue ≈ $223.3k.

Core formulas (SaaS unit-economics)
- ARPU (monthly) = Revenue_month / #active_customers_month
- Monthly gross contribution per customer = ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin
- Monthly churn (if you have annual churn): monthly_churn = 1 - (1 - annual_churn)^(1/12)
- LTV (simple, constant churn model) = (ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin) / monthly_churn
- CAC = (Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (# new customers acquired in same period)
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC  (target generally ≥ 3x)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly * Gross_Margin)  (target generally ≤ 12 months for growth-stage SaaS)

Worked examples (useful to include in your slides). I’ll show three ARPU profiles using revenue = $223.3k/month split across customers:

Assumptions I’ll use for examples:
- Gross margin = 80% (typical SaaS range 70–90%)
- Annual churn scenarios: 10% (good), 20% (typical), 30% (weak)
- monthly_churn computed from annual churn using the formula above

Customer count examples (derived from total monthly revenue):
- If ARPU = $5,000/mo → customers ≈ 45
- If ARPU = $2,000/mo → customers ≈ 112
- If ARPU = $500/mo → customers ≈ 447

Now LTV results (rounded):

1) ARPU = $5,000/mo → contribution = $5,000 * 0.80 = $4,000
- Annual churn 10% → monthly_churn ≈ 0.88% → LTV ≈ $4,000 / 0.0088 ≈ $455k
- Annual churn 20% → monthly_churn ≈ 1.84% → LTV ≈ $217k
- Annual churn 30% → monthly_churn ≈ 2.93% → LTV ≈ $137k

2) ARPU = $2,000/mo → contribution = $1,600
- 10% → LTV ≈ $1,600 / 0.0088 ≈ $182k
- 20% → LTV ≈ $87k
- 30% → LTV ≈ $47k

3) ARPU = $500/mo → contribution = $400
- 10% → LTV ≈ $45.5k
- 20% → LTV ≈ $21.7k
- 30% → LTV ≈ $11.7k

What those LTVs imply for CAC targets and payback
- Healthy CAC target (rule of thumb) = LTV / 3
- Payback months at that CAC = (LTV/3) / contribution

Example quick numbers (using the mid churn case 20% annual):
- ARPU $5k: LTV ≈ $217k → CAC target ≈ $72k → payback ≈ $72k / $4k ≈ 18 months (too long)
- ARPU $2k: LTV ≈ $87k → CAC target ≈ $29k → payback ≈ $29k / $1.6k ≈ 18 months
- ARPU $500: LTV ≈ $21.7k → CAC target ≈ $7.2k → payback ≈ $7.2k / $0.4k ≈ 18 months

(You’ll notice payback months here scale similarly because I used the 3x LTV:CAC rule — if you accept longer payback your allowable CAC increases; if you want sub-12-month payback you need lower CAC or higher contribution.)

Interpretation & actions to include in the finance narrative
- Reconcile runway/burn figures immediately in the deck — investors and board will ask. State whether runway is “as of today with current burn” or “projected runway including revenue and planned cuts.”
- LTV is very sensitive to churn and gross margin. Even a few % improvement in annual churn meaningfully increases LTV.
- CAC and payback drive fundraising and growth cadence decisions: if payback is >12 months you’ll need to either raise less often but for larger amounts, or increase capital efficiency.
- Practical levers: reduce churn (improve onboarding, customer success, expansion), increase gross margin (productize lower-cost delivery), raise price/packaging (increase ARPU), and reduce CAC (optimize paid channels, increase organic sales).
- If LTV:CAC is <3x or payback >12 months, include a remediation plan in the materials (cost cuts, retention plan, S&M reallocation, testing new channels).

What I need from you to compute your actual CAC & LTV
- Sales & Marketing spend (last 12 months and last quarter)
