- Total customers (active customers) or average revenue per customer (ARPU) so we can convert revenue to per-customer metrics.
- Gross margin (contribution margin) on revenue (typical SaaS: 70–80%).
- Customer churn rate (monthly or quarterly). If you don’t have churn, retention or lifetime in months, give an estimate.

Core formulas
- CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (New customers acquired in same period)
- ARPU (monthly) = Monthly revenue / Active customers
- Customer contribution per month = ARPU_monthly * Gross_margin
- LTV (simple, churn-based) = Contribution_per_month / Monthly_churn
  - Equivalent: LTV = (ARPU_monthly * Gross_margin) / churn_rate_monthly
- Payback period (months) = CAC / Contribution_per_month
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV / CAC
Guidance targets (common benchmarks)
- LTV:CAC: aim > 3x (>=3 is healthy; <3 means you’re spending a lot to acquire revenue)
- Payback period: ideally < 12 months (faster is better; <6 months is excellent for early-stage growth)
- Gross margin: for SaaS, 70–80% is typical; lower margins reduce LTV materially

Quick worked examples using your company numbers (given inputs: cash $5.2M, runway 30 months, burn $72K/month, revenue last quarter $670K)
- Monthly revenue = $670K / 3 = $223,333

I’ll show three ARPU scenarios (SMB / Mid-market / Enterprise), assume S&M spend = 20% of quarterly revenue (common for growth stage) => S&M per quarter = 670K * 0.20 = $134,000. Assume new customers acquired = 10% of base customers per quarter (this is an example growth rate). Gross margin = 75%.

Scenario A — Low ARPU (SMB)
- ARPU monthly = $200 → estimated active customers = 223,333 / 200 ≈ 1,117
- New customers (10% quarter) ≈ 112
- CAC = 134,000 / 112 ≈ $1,196
- Contribution per month = 200 * 0.75 = $150
- If monthly churn = 2% → LTV = 150 / 0.02 = $7,500 → LTV:CAC ≈ 6.3 → Payback ~ 7.97 months
- If monthly churn = 5% → LTV = 150 / 0.05 = $3,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.5 → Payback ~ 8 months

Scenario B — Mid ARPU
- ARPU monthly = $1,000 → active customers ≈ 223
- New customers ≈ 22
- CAC = 134,000 / 22 ≈ $6,091
- Contribution per month = 1,000 * 0.75 = $750
- Churn 2% → LTV = 750 / 0.02 = $37,500 → LTV:CAC ≈ 6.15 → Payback ~ 8.1 months
- Churn 5% → LTV = 15,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.46 → Payback ~ 8.1 months

Scenario C — High ARPU (Enterprise)
- ARPU monthly = $5,000 → active customers ≈ 45
- New customers ≈ 5
- CAC = 134,000 / 5 ≈ $26,800
- Contribution per month = 5,000 * 0.75 = $3,750
- Churn 2% → LTV = 3,750 / 0.02 = $187,500 → LTV:CAC ≈ 7.0 → Payback ~ 7.1 months
- Churn 5% → LTV = 75,000 → LTV:CAC ≈ 2.8 → Payback ~ 7.1 months

Key takeaways from these scenarios
- LTV:CAC is highly sensitive to churn. Reducing monthly churn from 5% to 2% multiplies LTV by ~2.5x.
- Payback period is determined by CAC relative to contribution margin; in the examples payback is ~7–8 months because I assumed 20% S&M spend and 10% net-new customers per quarter. If you spend more or acquire fewer customers, CAC and payback worsen.
- Benchmarks: these example LTV:CAC ratios look healthy if churn is low (~2%); if churn is higher (~5%) the ratio drops near or below the 3x target — that’s where the finance narrative should focus attention.

Suggested finance narrative (short)
- “Using last quarter revenue of $670K (monthly run-rate $223K) and current S&M run-rate (~20% of revenue), our blended CAC is in the $1k–$27k range depending on customer segment ARPU. With a 75% contribution margin and 2–3% monthly churn, our LTV:CAC is comfortably above the 3x threshold and payback is ~7–8 months. However, if monthly churn increases toward 4–5%, LTV:CAC falls to ~2–3x, raising acquisition payback risk. Priority: reduce churn (improve retention), track channel-level CAC, and optimize S&M spend to target segments that deliver LTV:CAC > 3x and payback < 12 months.”

