I can help — first a quick note and then a clear plan with formulas, examples, and a ready finance narrative you can paste into internal materials.

Quick inconsistency to confirm
- You wrote: cash = $5.2M, burn = $72K/month, runway = 30 months. Those three don’t match: 5.2M / 72K ≈ 72 months of runway, not 30. Two possibilities:
  - Your burn number is “gross” burn and runway cited uses a different (larger) net burn, or
  - The 30 months runway is correct and monthly burn is ~ $173K (5.2M / 30 ≈ 173K).
Please confirm which numbers are net vs gross and which runway you want to present.

Data I need (if you want exact CAC/LTV)
- Total sales & marketing (S&M) spend in the period you’ll use for CAC (quarter or month)
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- Total active customers (to compute ARPU), or ARPU / average contract value (ACV)
- Gross margin (or cost of service) %
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime
If you give those I’ll compute exact CAC, LTV, payback, and LTV:CAC.

Key formulas (use these or I’ll compute if you provide inputs)
- Monthly revenue = Revenue last quarter / 3 = 670,000 / 3 = $223,333 / month
- ARPU (monthly) = Monthly revenue / total active customers
- CAC = Total S&M spend (in period) / # of customers acquired in that period
- Customer lifetime (months) = 1 / monthly churn
- LTV = ARPU_monthly * gross_margin % * customer_lifetime
  (equivalently LTV = (ARPU_monthly * gross_margin) / monthly_churn)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly * gross_margin)
- LTV:CAC ratio = LTV / CAC

SaaS benchmarks to target
- LTV:CAC > 3 is healthy (>= 3 recommended)
- CAC payback < 12 months is good for fast growth; < 24 months acceptable for slower growth
- Gross margin for SaaS typically 70–85%

Example scenarios (illustrative — I used reasonable assumptions so you can see the mechanics). Change the customer counts and churn to suit your business.

Assumptions common to all examples:
- Monthly revenue = $223,333
- Gross margin = 80% (SaaS benchmark)
- S&M spend = 20% of revenue (quarterly S&M = 0.20 * 670K = $134K)
Scenario A — mid-sized customer base
- Active customers = 500 → ARPU_monthly = 223,333 / 500 = $447
- New customers in quarter = 50 → CAC = 134,000 / 50 = $2,680
- Monthly churn = 2% → customer lifetime = 1 / 0.02 = 50 months
- LTV = 447 * 0.80 * 50 = $17,880
- LTV:CAC = 17,880 / 2,680 ≈ 6.7 (very healthy)
- CAC payback = 2,680 / (447 * 0.80) ≈ 7.5 months

Scenario B — smaller customer base (higher ARPU)
- Active customers = 200 → ARPU_monthly = 223,333 / 200 = $1,117
- New customers in quarter = 20 → CAC = 134,000 / 20 = $6,700
- Monthly churn = 3% → lifetime = 33.3 months
- LTV = 1,117 * 0.80 * 33.3 ≈ $29,822
- LTV:CAC ≈ 29,822 / 6,700 ≈ 4.45 (healthy)
- CAC payback ≈ 6 months

Scenario C — high churn (worse case)
- Active customers = 500 (ARPU = $447)
- New customers in quarter = 50 (CAC = $2,680)
- Monthly churn = 5% → lifetime = 20 months
- LTV = 447 * 0.80 * 20 = $7,152
- LTV:CAC = 7,152 / 2,680 ≈ 2.7 (below target)
- CAC payback ≈ 2,680 / (447 * 0.80) ≈ 7.5 months (payback ok, but LTV too low)

How to present the finance narrative (short version you can paste)
- Current financial position: “Summit Software holds $5.2M in cash. (Confirm net burn and runway — see note.) Revenue last quarter was $670K (≈ $223K/mo).”
- Performance metrics & interpretation: “We calculate CAC as total S&M spend divided by new customers. LTV is ARPU * gross margin * expected lifetime. With benchmark assumptions (80% gross margin, 2–3% monthly churn), our LTV:CAC ratio is in the X–Y range and CAC payback is Z months. Target LTV:CAC is >=3 and CAC payback <12 months for scalable SaaS growth.”
- Strategic implications: “If LTV:CAC < 3, prioritize retention (reduce churn) and expand upsells to increase LTV; if CAC payback is >12 months, slow pay-to-acquire or find cheaper channels. Given current cash, we should align growth spend so net burn doesn’t cut runway below our fundraising or milestone timeline.”
- Ask/askables for leadership: “Confirm S&M spend, new customers per quarter, active customer count, and gross margin so we can produce exact CAC, LTV, and a one-page metrics dashboard (CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback, churn, ARPU).”

Recommended next steps (I can do these immediately)
1. Provide: quarterly S&M spend, # of new customers last quarter, total active customers, gross margin %, and churn (monthly or annual). I’ll compute exact CAC, LTV, payback, and produce a one-page slide-ready summary.
2. If you don’t have churn or customer counts, I’ll run sensitivity scenarios (best/likely/worst) so leadership can see ranges and what needs to change to hit target metrics.

Which would you like me to do now?
- A: Compute exact metrics — paste S&M, new customers, active customers, churn, gross margin.
- B: Run a set of sensitivity scenarios and produce a one-page metrics summary using reasonable benchmarks.

