Which is correct: 30 months runway or $72K/month burn? I’ll proceed assuming you’ll confirm which is right. I’ll also need customer and marketing data to calculate CAC and LTV precisely. Below I give the formulas, what inputs I need, a short set of target benchmarks, and several worked examples using reasonable assumptions so you can see how the finance narrative will look.

Key formulas
- CAC = (Sales & Marketing spend in a period) / (New customers acquired in that period)
- ARPU (average revenue per user) = (Revenue in period) / (Number of customers in that period)
  - If revenue given is quarterly, ARPU_monthly = (quarterly revenue / 3) / customers
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly churn rate
- LTV (simple unit economics) = ARPU_monthly * gross_margin% * (1 / monthly_churn)
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_monthly * gross_margin%)

Essential inputs I need from you (or tell me which I should assume)
- Number of active customers (or new customers in the quarter)
- New customers acquired (in the same period S&M spend is measured)
- Sales & Marketing spend (quarterly or monthly)
- Monthly churn rate (or annual churn)
- Gross margin (for your product)
- Confirm burn/runway (cash 5.2M, burn either $72K/month or ~$173K/month — please confirm)

Benchmarks / rules of thumb for SaaS finance narrative
- LTV : CAC target – ideally ≥ 3 : 1 (higher is better)
- CAC payback – ideally < 12 months (faster is safer), aggressive growth can tolerate longer if funded
- Gross margin – higher increases LTV (SaaS typically 70–90%)
- If LTV : CAC < 3 or payback > 12 months, you should justify higher customer lifetime, strategic reasons to spend for growth, or plan to reduce CAC

Worked examples (using your revenue figure to derive ARPU under different ARPU assumptions)
Your revenue last quarter = $670K → monthly revenue = $670K / 3 ≈ $223.3K

I’ll show three ARPU scenarios so you can see ranges:

Scenario A — Low ARPU ($200 / month)
- Monthly ARPU = $200
- Estimated customers = monthly revenue / ARPU = 223,333 / 200 ≈ 1,116 customers
- Assume gross margin = 80% and monthly churn = 3% (0.03)
- Customer lifetime = 1 / 0.03 ≈ 33.3 months
- LTV = 200 * 0.8 * 33.3 ≈ $5,333
- If you target LTV:CAC = 3, allowed CAC ≈ 5,333 / 3 ≈ $1,778
- CAC payback (if CAC = 1,778) = 1,778 / (200 * 0.8) = 1,778 / 160 ≈ 11.1 months

Scenario B — Mid ARPU ($500 / month)
- Customers ≈ 223,333 / 500 ≈ 447 customers
- Same GM 80%, churn 3%
- Lifetime ≈ 33.3 months
- LTV = 500 * 0.8 * 33.3 ≈ $13,333
- Target CAC (LTV:CAC=3) ≈ 13,333 / 3 ≈ $4,444
- CAC payback ≈ 4,444 / (500 * 0.8) = 4,444 / 400 ≈ 11.1 months

Scenario C — High ARPU ($1,000 / month)
- Customers ≈ 223,333 / 1,000 ≈ 223 customers
- LTV = 1,000 * 0.8 * 33.3 ≈ $26,667
- Target CAC ≈ 8,889
- CAC payback ≈ 8,889 / (1,000 * 0.8) = 8,889 / 800 ≈ 11.1 months

Notes about the examples:
- You’ll notice the payback months came out similar across ARPUs because payback = CAC / (ARPU*GM) and CAC is set to meet LTV:CAC = 3 — mathematically these tie out.
- If churn is lower (e.g., 1% monthly), lifetime becomes 100 months and LTV increases a lot, allowing much higher CAC or stronger LTV:CAC.
- If gross margin is lower, LTV falls proportionally.
- If S&M spend is front-loaded for growth intentionally, your finance narrative must justify longer payback or heavier burn against growth goals.

Immediate actions I can take for you
