   - Also revenue last quarter = $670k → monthly revenue ≈ $223k

Which of these is the correct “burn” definition? Is $72k the operating burn excluding revenue (or is it net burn after revenue)? Please confirm the correct burn/runway pair so the finance narrative is internally consistent.

What I need from you to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Sales & Marketing spend over the period you want to measure CAC (quarter, trailing 12 months, etc.)
- Number of NEW customers acquired in the same period
- Total number of active customers (or monthly/quarterly customer counts)
- Churn (monthly or annual %), or retention rates
- Gross margin (or COGS) % (for contribution-margin LTV)
- If you prefer, indicate the time window you want (last quarter, last 12 months)

Formulas I’ll use (and why)
- CAC = Total S&M spend (period) / New customers acquired (same period)
- ARPA (monthly) = Monthly revenue / Active customers
- Monthly churn = customers lost in month / customers at start of month
- Avg customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 / monthly churn
- LTV (contribution margin basis) = ARPA * gross_margin / monthly_churn
- LTV : CAC = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / (ARPA * gross_margin)

Quick illustrative scenarios using your revenue number (so you can see sensitivity)
Assumptions used below: monthly revenue = $670k / 3 = $223k.

Example A — mid-sized accounts
- Assume active customers = 100 → ARPA ≈ $2,233/month
- Gross margin = 70% → contribution per customer ≈ $1,563/month
- Monthly churn = 3% → avg lifetime ≈ 33 months
- LTV = 1,563 / 0.03 ≈ $52,100

If CAC = $6,000:
- LTV:CAC ≈ 52,100 / 6,000 ≈ 8.7x (very strong)
- CAC payback ≈ 6,000 / 1,563 ≈ 3.8 months

Example B — fewer, larger customers
- Active customers = 50 → ARPA ≈ $4,467/month
- Same GM 70%, churn 5% → contribution = 3,127/month; lifetime = 20 months
- LTV = 3,127 / 0.05 ≈ $62,540

If CAC = $12,000:
- LTV:CAC ≈ 62,540 / 12,000 ≈ 5.2x
- Payback ≈ 12,000 / 3,127 ≈ 3.8 months

Example C — many small customers
- Active customers = 200 → ARPA ≈ $1,116/month
- GM 70%, churn 4% → contribution = 781/month; lifetime = 25 months
- LTV = 781 / 0.04 ≈ $19,525

If CAC = $3,000:
- LTV:CAC ≈ 19,525 / 3,000 ≈ 6.5x
- Payback ≈ 3,000 / 781 ≈ 3.8 months

Key takeaways and benchmarks for your finance narrative
- LTV:CAC benchmark: ~3:1 is a typical target (higher is better but check for underinvestment if it’s extremely high).
- CAC payback target: <12 months is conservative; 6–12 months is common for growth-stage SaaS.
- LTV should be measured on contribution margin (so include gross margin); using revenue-only overstates value.
- Provide sensitivity analysis in the deck: show LTV and payback under different churn and ARPA assumptions.
- Reconcile burn/runway in the narrative: show net burn (cash outflows minus operational cash inflows) so runway math matches cash and revenue.

