1) Quick check of the numbers you gave
- Cash: $5.2M
- Runway: 30 months
- Burn rate: $72K / month
- Revenue last quarter: $670K → monthly revenue ≈ $670K / 3 = $223.3K

These don’t all line up. If your runway truly is 30 months with $5.2M cash, implied net burn is:
- Implied net burn = cash / runway = $5,200,000 / 30 ≈ $173,333 / month

But you said burn is $72K/month. If net burn is $72K, runway would be 5.2M / 72K ≈ 72 months. So please confirm:
- Which “burn” number is your net burn (expenses minus revenue) vs gross burn (total cash outflow)?
- Or is the 30-month runway calculated after an expected fundraising or including other cash inflows?

2) Key formulas (exact)
- CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) = Total Sales & Marketing spend during period ÷ Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- ARPU (monthly) = Monthly revenue ÷ Total customers
- Monthly churn rate = customers lost in month ÷ customers at start of month
- Customer lifetime (months) ≈ 1 ÷ monthly churn (for steady-state cohorts)
- LTV (simple, contribution-margin based) = ARPU × Customer lifetime × Gross margin
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV ÷ CAC
- CAC payback period (months) = CAC ÷ (ARPU × Gross margin)

Notes: For subscription businesses you can refine LTV using discounting or cohort-based retention curves; for non-subscription, use purchase frequency and gross margin per purchase.

3) Illustrative scenarios (showing sensitivity)
I’ll use your monthly revenue = $223,333 and choose plausible customer & S&M assumptions to demonstrate how LTV and CAC vary.

Assumed S&M spend (example): $100K / month, new customers acquired = 80 / month → CAC = $100K / 80 = $1,250

Scenario A — conservative
- Total customers = 1,200 → ARPU = 223,333 / 1,200 = $186 / month
- Monthly churn = 4% → lifetime ≈ 25 months
- Gross margin = 70%
- LTV = 186 × 25 × 0.70 ≈ $3,257
- LTV:CAC = 3,257 / 1,250 ≈ 2.61
- CAC payback = 1,250 ÷ (186 × 0.70) ≈ 9.6 months

Scenario B — optimistic / higher ARPU, low churn
- Total customers = 450 → ARPU = 223,333 / 450 ≈ $496 / month
- Monthly churn = 2% → lifetime ≈ 50 months
- Gross margin = 80%
- LTV = 496 × 50 × 0.80 ≈ $19,852
- LTV:CAC = 19,852 / 1,250 ≈ 15.9
- CAC payback = 1,250 ÷ (496 × 0.80) ≈ 3.2 months

Takeaway from examples: small changes in ARPU, churn, or gross margin dramatically change LTV and the LTV:CAC ratio. That’s why accurate cohort-level churn and S&M spend per channel are critical.

4) Benchmarks & what to aim for
- LTV : CAC > 3 is a common healthy target for SaaS; >5 is excellent.
- CAC payback < 12 months is typically good; < 6 months is excellent for high-growth situations.
- Gross margin assumptions matter — subscription software often uses 70–90% GM depending on hosting & support cost.

