Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — first I’ll outline what I need, then give the formulas and a few worked examples so you can see the impact of different assumptions. After you confirm key inputs (or give me the data), I’ll compute CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, payback, and a short finance narrative you can use.

Questions / data I need from you
- Number of customers at the end of the last quarter (or customer count by cohort / month if available).
- Number of new customers acquired during the period you want to measure CAC for (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months).
- Sales & marketing spend for the same period used for CAC (total S&M costs; include hiring, commissions, marketing, events).
- Average revenue per customer (ARPU) per month or per quarter, or customer-level ARR if you have it.
- Gross margin on revenue (or cost of goods sold as a % of revenue).
- Customer churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime (months or years).
- Whether "burn rate $72K/month" is net burn (cash decrease/month) or operating cash burn (before revenue). Also confirm the 30-month runway figure (I’ll reconcile below).

Key formulas (simple, standard)
- CAC = (Total Sales & Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that period)
- ARPU (monthly) = (Revenue per month) / (Number of customers)
- Churn rate (monthly) = fraction of customers lost per month
- LTV (subscription/simple) = ARPU / monthly churn * Gross margin
  - Equivalent: LTV = ARPU * (Average customer lifetime in months) * Gross margin
- LTV:CAC = LTV / CAC
- CAC payback (months) = CAC / ARPU

Runway & burn — quick reconciliation
You gave:
- Cash = $5.2M
- Runway = 30 months
- Stated burn = $72K/month
- Revenue last quarter = $670K → ≈ $223.3K/month

Check 1 — implied net burn from runway:
- Implied monthly net cash burn = 5,200,000 / 30 ≈ $173,333/month
Check 2 — if burn is $72K/month, runway = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72 months

So the numbers conflict. Possible explanations:
- $72K is operating cash burn excluding something (e.g., it might be “operating loss” after offsets or excludes certain non-cash items), or
- The 30-month runway assumes a planned increase in spend (hiring, product launches), or revenue decline, or reserved capital, or includes committed financing obligations.

If your monthly revenue is $223K and your net cash burn is $173K/month (implied by 30 months runway):
- That implies total monthly cash outflows ≈ revenue + net burn = 223K + 173K ≈ $396K/month (i.e., total cash usage), which is plausible if you run ~\$400K/month in OpEx and collect ~$223K in revenue.

Worked examples (illustrative — I’ll replace with real inputs when you provide them)
Base numbers from you:
- Monthly revenue = $223,333 (670K / 3)
- Cash = $5.2M

Example A — small-business customer base (illustrative)
- Assume customers = 500
  - ARPU monthly = 223,333 / 500 = $446.67
- Assume S&M last quarter = 20% of revenue = 0.20 * 670K = $134K → CAC over quarter depends on new customers
- Assume new customers in quarter = 50 → CAC = 134,000 / 50 = $2,680
- Assume monthly churn = 2% → average lifetime = 1 / 0.02 = 50 months
- Assume gross margin = 70%
  - LTV = ARPU / churn * GM = 446.67 / 0.02 * 0.70 = 15,667
  - LTV:CAC = 15,667 / 2,680 ≈ 5.8
  - CAC payback = CAC / ARPU = 2,680 / 446.67 ≈ 6 months

Example B — enterprise customers (fewer customers, higher ARPU)
- Assume customers = 100
  - ARPU monthly = 223,333 / 100 = 2,233
- Same S&M assumptions, new customers in quarter = 10 → CAC = 134,000 / 10 = 13,400
- Monthly churn = 1% → lifetime = 100 months; GM = 80%
  - LTV = 2,233 / 0.01 * 0.80 = 178,640
  - LTV:CAC = 178,640 / 13,400 ≈ 13.3
  - CAC payback = 13,400 / 2,233 ≈ 6 months

These examples show how sensitive results are to customer count, churn, S&M spend and gross margin.

What I recommend for the finance narrative
- Reconcile runway definition: explicitly state whether runway uses current burn (expenses − revenue) or pro-forma (planned spend). Show the math so board/investors understand the assumptions.
- Present unit economics: ARPU, CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, and CAC payback in a single slide/table with assumptions used.
- Show cohort analysis if possible: cohort LTV curves (3, 6, 12 months) and payback by cohort — this demonstrates retention and the trend in CAC over time.
- Show sensitivity scenarios (conservative / base / aggressive) for churn and CAC — small changes in churn hugely affect LTV.
- Use benchmarks: LTV:CAC ~3:1 is commonly cited as a good target; payback ideally < 12 months for high-growth companies, shorter for higher risk aversion.
- If you have long runway but weak unit economics, explain tradeoffs: runway buys time to fix unit economics (improve retention, increase prices, reduce CAC).

Next steps (I can do any of these)
- If you give: customers, new customers, S&M spend, churn, gross margin — I’ll compute CAC, ARPU, LTV, LTV:CAC, payback and produce a one-page summary and a short finance narrative.
- If you want, I can also prepare a 3-scenario sensitivity table (churn ±1%, CAC ±20%, gross margin variations) and a short slide-ready paragraph.

Tell me which data you can provide now (or say “use assumptions” and pick a scenario), and I’ll run the calculations and produce the narrative.

